    "snippet": "---\ntitle: William Golding - Wikipedia\nauthor: Authority control databases\ndate: 2002-01-21\n---\nname: William Golding\nhonorific_prefix: Sir\nhonorific_suffix: GBR 100% CBE FRSL\nbirth_name: William Gerald Golding\nbirth_date: 19 September 1911\nbirth_place: Newquay, Cornwall, UK\ndeath_date: 19 June 1993\ndeath_place: Perranarworthal, Cornwall, UK\nresting_place: Holy Trinity Church, Bowerchalke, Wiltshire, England\noccupation: Schoolteacher, novelist, playwright, poet\nalma_mater: Brasenose College, Oxford\ngenre: Survivalist fiction, robinsonade, adventure, sea story, science fiction, essay, historical fiction, stageplay, poetry\nnotableworks: Lord of the Flies, Rites of Passage\nawards: ||}}\nsignature: William Golding signature.jpg\n\nSir William Gerald Golding (19 September 1911 \u2013 19 June 1993) was a British novelist, playwright, and poet. Best known for his debut novel Lord of the Flies (1954), Golding published another 12 volumes of fiction in his lifetime. In 1980, Golding was awarded the Booker Prize for Rites of Passage, the first novel in what became his sea trilogy, To the Ends of the Earth. He was awarded the 1983 Nobel Prize in Literature.\n\nAs a result of his contributions to literature, Golding was knighted in 1988.William Golding: Awards . William Golding.co.uk. Retrieved 17 June 2012 He was also a fellow of the Royal Society of Literature. In 2008, The Times ranked Golding third on its list of \"The 50 greatest British writers since 1945\".The 50 greatest British writers since 1945. The Times (5 January 2008). Retrieved on 1 February 2010.\n\nBiography\n\nEarly life\n\nSon of Alec Golding, a science master at Marlborough Grammar School (1905 to retirement), and Mildred, n\u00e9e Curnoe,Raychel Haugrud Reiff, William Golding: Lord of the Flies, Marshall Cavendish, 2009 William Golding was born at his maternal grandmother's house, 47 Mount Wise, Newquay,Carey, Chap. 5 ('Childhood'), pg."
