    "score": 13.223381495138554,
    "snippet": "---\ntitle: Student Profiles\ndate: 2019-05-01\n---\nClass of 2026\n\nMilena Soci\n\nMilena Soci is an Italian journalist with Cuban roots, born in Modena in 2001. She began her career by creating an independent blog and later worked as a contributor for the Italian newspaper Il Resto del Carlino, gaining hands-on experience in newsroom reporting. She holds a double-degree in Modern Literature from the University of Bologna and Sorbonne Universit\u00e9, where she focused her studies on French literature and journalism, including a semester in Paris.\n\nHer undergraduate thesis, titled \"Open Source Intelligence in Bellingcat's Newsroom: Images of the Syrian Civil War During the Gaza Conflict,\" explored the use of OSINT techniques, reverse image search, and in-depth source verification in investigative journalism.\n\nMilena is fluent in Italian, French, English, and Spanish.\n\nAlongside her journalistic work, she published her debut short story, La Disattesa, which was a semifinalist at the 2023 Campiello Giovani Prize, and later released her first novel, Lavanda. At NYU, she aims to strengthen her audiovisual skills to explore innovative forms of investigative journalism, focusing on uncovering social injustices, corruption, and human rights violations.\n\nSam Wands\n\nSam is from Denver, CO, where he grew up tinkering with his parents' and grandparents' old cameras, fascinated by the idea of capturing the world around him. His studies then took him to Los Angeles, CA, where he graduated from the University of Southern California with a BA in International Relations and French, and then a Master of Public Policy. In high school and college, Sam studied abroad in Turkey, Belgium, and at Sciences Po in Paris, France. Sam then lived in San Francisco, CA, and Washington, DC, working on health care policy projects and state government lobbying, respectively.\n\nNow, Sam hopes to reconnect with his inner child who dreamed of being a documentarian. Combining his background in public policy and foreign languages with journalism, Sam plans to use documentary as a means to reach the greatest number of people possible when telling the important stories of our times. His driving vision is to elevate the voices of those who never thought they would be heard and to create bridges between seemingly disparate cultures. Sam speaks Italian, French, and Portuguese and hopes to learn more while exploring different corners of the world.\n\nElla Jane Simmons graduated with honors from the University of Mississippi in 2025, receiving a B"
  },
